1. RAID (Redundant Array of Independent Disks)-
Single disk failure causes data loss. RAID combines multiple disks to improve:
- Performance
- Reliability
- Capacity

Imagine this scenario ->

You have one hard drive -> Disk A
It stores customer data.One day the disk fails -> Everything is gone.

RAID solves this problem ->

a. RAID 0 (Performance) (Striping)
- Data split across disks.

Suppose a file is: ABCDEFGHIJ
Instead of storing all on one disk:

Disk1 : ACEGI
Disk2 : BDFHJ

Both disks work simultaneously.

Benefit:
- Data is read twice as fast.
- Like having two cashiers instead of one.

Problem:
- If either disk dies:

Disk1 dies
You only have:

BDFHJ

File is unusable.

b. RAID 1 (Mirroring)

Store the same thing twice.

Disk1 : ABCDE
Disk2 : ABCDE

Benefit:
- If Disk1 dies:
Disk2 still works.

Real Life Example:
Google Photos stored on two different disks.

One fails.

User doesn't notice.

c. RAID 5 - Uses striping + parity. Very common.

Three disks.

Disk1 : Data
Disk2 : Data
Disk3 : Parity (Parity is like a mathematical checksum)

If one disk fails: Missing disk reconstructed using remaining disks.

Example

Think of three friends remembering a phone number.

Even if one forgets, the other two can help recover it.

d. Why RAID 6 Exists: Like RAID 5 but stores two parity blocks.
- Can survive 2 disk failures.
- Preferred for large storage systems.

Modern disks are huge.

10TB+

Rebuilding a failed disk can take many hours.

While rebuilding: 2nd disk fails
- RAID 5 loses data.
- RAID 6 survives two failures.

This is why enterprise systems prefer RAID 6.

e. RAID 10: RAID 1 + RAID 0

Mirror Pair 1
Mirror Pair 2

Then striped together.

Excellent performance. 
Excellent reliability.
Expensive.

